title: Storage wear
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: How worn the store is — what writes left pending, and the ids no metarecord holds — so that a client can say when a fold, or a reindex, is worth it.

Two things make a store slower than a freshly built one, and they are cured by
different means. `KvStore::wear` measures both, in one read of the pending lists and
two counters, and `GET /repos/:repo/wear` ([[GET /repos/:repo/wear]]) serves it, and `mf repo wear` prints it (`--json` for the answer as it is). That command is not the user's activity: reading the wear is how one waits for the idle fold ([[Folding in idle time]]), so it must not hold the fold back. No threshold says when a fold or a reindex is ''advised''; none has been measured.

!! What is pending: a fold cures it

|!Field |!Means |
|`mass_entries`, `mass_parts`, `mass_bytes` |entries of the parts' lists ([[Mass writes in the store]]), the parts they are in, their size |
|`events`, `event_fields`, `event_bytes` |pending events of the fields' values, the fields, their size |
|`trigrams_pending` |ids whose trigrams a fold has still to build ([[Text search in the store]]) |

Every read of a record pays for the entries of its part and every read of a field's
values for the field's events, so the cost of reading grows with these. The idle fold
([[Folding in idle time]]) writes them in; nothing else limits them.

!! Ids nobody holds: a reindex cures it

`ids_allocated` is the number of dense ids ever handed out, `ids_live` those of
metarecords that exist; `dead_ids` is the difference — deleted metarecords, and ids reserved for a uuid
something points at ([[Store tables]]) — and `dead_fraction` its share. A deleted
metarecord's id stays in every bitmap, and its record in the store, until a reindex
numbers the metarecords again ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]), so bitmaps hold more ids than they
have holders, and a subtree's ids are scattered over the years.

No threshold is set here: when a fold or a reindex is ''worth'' advising is for the
client, which will have measured.
